ARM: dts: pyra: let the charger regulate the cell to 4.19 V

The bq2429x driver sets the charge voltage (REG04 VREG) at probe to

    min(vsys regulator-max-microvolt - 150 mV, battery voltage-max-design)

because VSYS can sit up to 150 mV above a full cell while running from
VBUS.  With the vsys maximum at 4.2 V that is 4.05 V, which the 16 mV
encoding rounds down to 4.048 V, so every Pyra kernel stops charging
about 150 mV short of the cell's 4.2 V (gauge about 87 % at
termination).

Every part fitted on VSYS on a standard (non-4G) V5.x board (main, CPU
and display boards) is rated for 4.35 V or more: the tightest are the TS3A227E jack detector
(VDD 2.5-4.5 V recommended, 5 V absolute) and the WL1837MOD (VBAT
2.9-4.8 V).  The bq24297 itself bounds VSYS at 4.35 V (VSYS_MAX, with
VBAT up to 4.2 V and the BATFET off), and its own power-on default
VREG of 4.208 V already lets VSYS reach that while charging before
Linux probes.

Raise the vsys maximum to 4.35 V.  The battery's 4.2 V then caps VREG:
(4200000 - 3504000) / 16000 = 43 steps = 4.192 V.  Nothing else reads
the maximum: the vsys regulator's set_voltage is a no-op and its
get_voltage reports VSYSMIN (3.5 V), which stays inside the range.

Not for 4G boards: the modem runs from VSYS and switches itself off at
4.25 V, which is why the vsys maximum is 4.2 V (hns, forum posts 1698207
and 1719755).  At 4.192 V VREG, VSYS can reach about 4.29 V while
charging.  The DTs do not tell 4G boards apart, so this needs a non-4G
variant or a boot-time modem check before any image carries it.

DRAFT: not applied to any kernel until the owner decides.
---
--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/pyra-mainboard-common.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/pyra-mainboard-common.dtsi
@@ -567,7 +567,7 @@
 				regulator-compatible = "bq2429x-vsys";
 				regulator-name = "vsys";
 				regulator-min-microvolt = <3500000>;
-				regulator-max-microvolt = <4200000>;
+				regulator-max-microvolt = <4350000>;
 				regulator-always-on;
 				regulator-boot-on;
 			};
